{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for him and his seed after him.</b> It would appear from this that had he not performed this deed, his priesthood would not have been established as an eternal priesthood — and how is this possible? Was not the priesthood given to Aaron and his sons, and were they not all anointed with the anointing oil, so that every priest they beget is included among the priests? But the matter was thus: Phinehas was born before the anointing of Aaron and his sons, and the priesthood includes only those sons born to them after their anointing. If so, Phinehas, who was born before their anointing, was not consecrated as a priest and did not enter the category of priesthood, and through this deed that he performed the priesthood was given to him forever. This is the meaning of 'an eternal priesthood,' and this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'Phinehas was not consecrated as a priest until he slew Zimri.'" "<b>And the sage R. Abraham, of blessed memory, wrote</b> that the High Priests were all descendants of Phinehas, and the word אחריו ('after him') is a sign that he died and is not Elijah — end quote. But the truth is in accordance with the opinion of the Rabbis, of blessed memory, that Phinehas is Elijah, and the meaning of אחריו is 'after his departure.'" "<b>A covenant of an eternal priesthood.</b> He did not associate salt with him, as He associated it with Aaron and all his seed, because they were promised in two worlds — which are the realm of the covenant and of permanence — since they are sustained in this world from the high table, which is the gifts, and they shall be the inheritance of HaShem in the world to come. And so it is written (Numbers 18:20): ״אני חלקך ונחלתך״ — 'I am your portion and your inheritance'; 'I am your portion' in this world, and 'your inheritance' in the world to come. Therefore He associated salt with them, for salt encompasses covenant and permanence. But here, with Phinehas, who draws power from חסד (lovingkindness), as hinted in his name — פינחס, 'פני חסד' (the face of lovingkindness), which is the attribute of the priest — He did not associate salt with him, for He promised him eternal permanence and that death would have no dominion over him." "<b>And he atoned for the children of Israel.</b> To teach you that one who sheds the blood of the wicked is as though he offered a sacrifice. And so it is written (Exodus 21:14): ״מעם מזבחי תקחנו למות״ — 'from My altar you shall take him to die' — even if he is a priest engaged in the service upon the altar, from there you shall take him to death."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The text reads 'וכתב' without a bold lemma of its own; it appears to continue directly from the preceding comment's final word. Rendered as a bolded opening following the pattern of the series."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:11.055864941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}